DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     Displays the detailed health status for the given pools.  If no _p_o_o_l is
     specified, then the status of each pool in the system is displayed.  For
     more information on pool and device health, see the _D_e_v_i_c_e _F_a_i_l_u_r_e _a_n_d
     _R_e_c_o_v_e_r_y section of zpoolconcepts(7).

     If a scrub or resilver is in progress, this command reports the percent‐
     age done and the estimated time to completion.  Both of these are only
     approximate, because the amount of data in the pool and the other work‐
     loads on the system can change.

     ----ppoowweerr
             Display vdev enclosure slot power status (on or off).

     --cc [_S_C_R_I_P_T_1[,_S_C_R_I_P_T_2]â¦]
             Run a script (or scripts) on each vdev and include the output as
             a new column in the zzppooooll ssttaattuuss output.  See the --cc option of
             zzppooooll iioossttaatt for complete details.

     --DD      Display a histogram of deduplication statistics, showing the al‐
             located (physically present on disk) and referenced (logically
             referenced in the pool) block counts and sizes by reference
             count.

     --ee      Only show unhealthy vdevs (not-ONLINE or with errors).

     --gg      Display vdev GUIDs instead of the normal device names These GUIDs
             can be used in place of device names for the zpool detach/off‐
             line/remove/replace commands.

     --ii      Display vdev initialization status.

     --LL      Display real paths for vdevs resolving all symbolic links.  This
             can be used to look up the current block device name regardless
             of the _/_d_e_v_/_d_i_s_k_/ path used to open it.

     --pp      Display numbers in parsable (exact) values.

     --PP      Display full paths for vdevs instead of only the last component
             of the path.  This can be used in conjunction with the --LL flag.

     --ss      Display the number of leaf vdev slow I/O operations.  This is the
             number of I/O operations that didn't complete in zziioo__ssllooww__iioo__mmss
             milliseconds (3300000000 by default).  This does not necessarily mean
             the I/O operations failed to complete, just took an unreasonably
             long amount of time.  This may indicate a problem with the under‐
             lying storage.

     --tt      Display vdev TRIM status.

     --TT uu|dd  Display a time stamp.  Specify uu for a printed representation of
             the internal representation of time.  See time(1).  Specify dd for
             standard date format.  See date(1).

     --vv      Displays verbose data error information, printing out a complete
             list of all data errors since the last complete pool scrub.  If
             the head_errlog feature is enabled and files containing errors
             have been removed then the respective filenames will not be re‐
             ported in subsequent runs of this command.

     --xx      Only display status for pools that are exhibiting errors or are
             otherwise unavailable.  Warnings about pools not using the latest
             on-disk format will not be included.

